{
  "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
  "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nDAVID A. MORAN, ESQ., Detroit, Michigan; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nTIMOTHY A. BAUGHMAN, ESQ., Detroit, Michigan; on behalf of the Respondent.\nDAVID B. SALMONS, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MORAN: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: For centuries the knock and announce rule has been a core part of the right of the people to be secure in their houses from unreasonable searches and seizures. It reflects the notion that when the government has the right to enter a house, whether to perform an arrest, to search for evidence, or to seize goods, that the people should have the right to answer the door in a dignified manner, except in an emergency, and to avoid the unnecessarily gratuitous embarrassment and shock that often follows a precipitous police entry."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Mr. Moran, how -- how long do you think the officers had to wait before they could have entered?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0000-0006",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MORAN: 49 of the 50 States currently suppress evidence following knock and announce violations, just as this Court did in -- in Miller and Sabbath."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Would it have been possible for these police to get a no-knock warrant?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. MORAN",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Michigan, there is no statute governing no-knock warrants, and there's -- there are also no court decisions governing no-knock warrants. And there never will be under the People v. Stevens regime."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Isn't a civil lawsuit bringable?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. MORAN",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Michigan has a particularly vigorous State immunity statute that makes it effectively impossible to sue for a -- a knock and announce violation. I have not found a single Michigan case in which anyone has successfully sued for a knock and announce violation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Criminal Justice Legal Foundation",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0001-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Criminal Justice Legal Foundation, one of the amici for the other side, concedes that tort remedies cannot, at this time, substitute for the exclusionary rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: There's -- there's specific knowledge."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Prosecutor",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BAUGHMAN: I would be happy to -- to, in a different case, make the argument that although Richards says a court cannot say that knock and announce is forgiven every time a drug warrant is executed on the theory that experience teaches that drugs and guns go together. I'd be happy to argue that that holding does not apply when a judge determines, in issuing the warrant, that drugs and guns go together, so I'm putting it in the warrant. I'd be happy to argue that case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: May I ask this?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Is there any evidence that the citizens -- that Michiganders are less litigious than people in other States?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Isn't -- isn't the problem that in -- in fact, it's the product of both?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David B. Salmon",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0002-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF DAVID B. SALMONS ON BEHALF OF THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There's no doubt in here that an invasion of the home was authorized by the warrant. Right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE BREYER",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Correct? I'm sorry. That is correct? I -- I thought that this warrant does not say you can enter the house without knocking."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE BREYER",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What -- what happens with my example? I'm curious. That's not a rhetorical question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE SOUTER",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is that not the general rule?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE STEVENS",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If you'd been the prosecutor in this case and you had -- knew that the evidence would be suppressed if there were a constitutional violation, would you have conceded that there was a constitutional violation in this case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE KENNEDY",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can you tell me what -- what happens if there's a violation of the daytime warrant provision in -- in a search warrant and the search is at night? Do we suppress?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You think there -- you think there was a violation of the knock and announce rule in this case. Correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. MORAN",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0010",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I do, Your Honor. The warrant was never actually made part of the record, but my understanding, from the record we have, was that only drugs -- there was only knowledge of drugs. The -- the warrant authorized a search for guns because Officer Good told the magistrate that in his experience guns were often associated with drugs. But they had no particularized information about any guns on the premises."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So you draw a distinction between two cases? If they illegally entered and they suddenly said we waited 4 seconds, it was supposed to be 15. They say, never mind. They go back out. There's another knock. They wait 15 and they come in. Then it's all right. Correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2005-04-1360",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e7b59737b7c64b5ddf354829f93b253e3bd96eaa0393b548f0173bd9044ae5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e7b59737b7-0003-0011",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
